Level 2 is the right gate. Planning is complete, no creative phase was required, and both executable units put tests before production code. The prose edit to `docs/contributing/preparation.md` correctly has no test. The direct read-only connect is the right exception to the warehouse chokepoint: `warehouse_path` and `open_current` call `home_dir()`, which creates the home, and `open_current` refuses a behind-head file. `duckdb.connect(path, read_only=True)` on a missing file raises and does not create it. A plain read-only connect works in the same process after a `storage_compatibility_version` connection has been closed; it raises `ConnectionException` only while that other connection is still open. The check-record line stays out of this milestone. Nothing in `sr-initialize` parses the full fact list, so new fact lines do not break it.

The plan has to change before build because existing probe tests will open this checkout's live warehouse. `probe_facts` today only resolves the home. After this plan it stats `warehouse.duckdb` and, when the file exists and the lock is free, opens it read-only. These callers do not set `STOCKROOM_HOME`: `_probe` in `skills/sr-search/tests/test_doctor.py` (the OS, GPU, torch, and engine-dir tests) and `test_probe_exits_zero_and_prints_fact_keys` in `test_doctor_cli.py`. On this machine that home already has the live warehouse. The plan says new tests use a temp home, and it also says the existing CLI key check stays and can see this machine's warehouse. The same plan says tests do not use the live warehouse and that no test opens that file. Those cannot all stay once probe opens the file.

Fix: point those existing callers at a temp home with no warehouse file. Do not do that inside `_probe` itself. `STOCKROOM_HOME` wins over `XDG_DATA_HOME`, and `test_probe_reports_home_and_source_from_resolve_home` plus `test_probe_home_facts_do_not_mkdir` already isolate the home by deleting `STOCKROOM_HOME` and setting `XDG_DATA_HOME`. Leave those two alone. Keep `test_probe_exits_zero_and_prints_fact_keys` a subset of the current keys and exit 0.

Advisory. The happy-path setup must drop the writer connection, not only close it. `warehouse.open` holds `.warehouse.lock` until the connection object is finalized. `close()` leaves that lock held while the variable still references the connection; a later `gc.collect()` after `del` releases it. A second `fcntl.flock(..., LOCK_EX | LOCK_NB)` in the same process blocks when that lock is held, so an in-process busy test is valid and does not need another process. If the happy-path test still holds the writer connection, probe reports `warehouse: busy` and never reads blocks. Close the lock fd after the busy check either way, including when the non-blocking acquire succeeds, so probe itself does not keep the writer lock while it connects.

Advisory. Count one set of block ids, unioned across base tables. On a warehouse with a few message rows the per-table distinct counts summed to 6 and the union was `{-1, 1, 3, 5}`. The behavior line is that one distinct count. `block_id` `-1` is DuckDB's sentinel for a constant validity segment: `persistent` is true and it is not a file block. Counting it overstates the live size by one `block_size` (262144 bytes here). Reclaimable on a fresh writer-opened file stayed non-negative (file 798720, two ids, reclaimable 274432). `additional_block_ids` was empty on a multi-megabyte text insert, so it did not change that figure. `PRAGMA storage_info` on `session_token_usage`, and on every other view, raises `CatalogException`. Iterate `duckdb_tables()` in `main` only. One view in the loop, caught as a pragma failure, turns a healthy file into `warehouse: unavailable`. `PRAGMA database_size` and `pragma_database_size()` both expose `used_blocks`, `free_blocks`, and `block_size`. `FROM pragma_database_size` without parentheses does not.

Advisory. The behavior list defines `warehouse: absent` only when the home directory does not exist. When the home exists and `warehouse.duckdb` does not, stat the file and do not connect. A read-only connect of a missing file raises and creates nothing; catching that error and printing `warehouse: unavailable` is a different fact from `absent`.

Advisory. The single structural change worth making, and not made here: give milestone 6 a function instead of a fact string. `live_bytes(con) -> int` unions persistent `block_id` values from `pragma_storage_info` over `duckdb_tables()` in `main`, drops ids below 0, and unions `additional_block_ids` when that list is non-empty, then multiplies by `block_size`. `storage_facts` prints `reclaimable-bytes` as the file size minus that integer. The check's low-disk test calls `live_bytes`. It does not parse the fact line and it does not grow a second copy of the pragma loop. The sentinel and the extra-id list are the part that differs from the formula written in the plan; the fresh-file measurement above is the cost of leaving them out.
